<section aria-labelledby="name-h">
  <h2 id="name-h" class="type-h2">The name</h2>
  <p><em>Arche</em> is the Greek word for a beginning, and for a first principle: the starting point that everything else follows from. It is pronounced AR-kee. I chose it because this system starts from a few rules and lets everything else follow, instead of starting from a pile of components.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="brand-h">
  <h2 id="brand-h" class="type-h2">How the system carries my brand</h2>
  <p>My brand has five personality traits: systematic, grounded, generous, clean and principled. Here is how I read each one in the system.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Systematic.</strong> Colors are chosen by job, not by value. A component asks for “text on the blue band”, never for a hex code.</li>
    <li><strong>Grounded.</strong> In the default mode, blue does the structural work. Yellow and red stay sparing, and each has one purpose.</li>
    <li><strong>Generous.</strong> Every component page shows its states, its HTML and its do and don’t rules, so someone else can use it without asking me.</li>
    <li><strong>Clean.</strong> A grid, square corners, two typefaces, and one job per type style.</li>
    <li><strong>Principled.</strong> Accessibility is the floor. Norway Red has one narrow job and is never on text, controls or validation.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="modes-h">
  <h2 id="modes-h" class="type-h2">Five color modes</h2>
  <p>Arche is one system with five color modes. Each mode takes its character from one of the four national traditions in my heritage, Swiss, Norwegian, Swedish and Greek, plus a default. A mode changes only the semantic color roles. Typography, spacing, grid, corner radius, motion and component structure are the same in every mode, and status colors such as Alert never change.</p>
