<section aria-labelledby="rules-h">
  <h2 id="rules-h" class="type-h2">Four rules the system rests on</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Components use role tokens, never raw colors.</strong> A button asks for <code>--arche-interactive-button-primary-fill-default</code>, not for a blue. That is how dark mode works without touching a component, and how the color modes work too.</li>
    <li><strong>One style, one job.</strong> Each type style does a single thing. When something needs emphasis, I change the weight inside the style instead of inventing a size between two others.</li>
    <li><strong>Color is never the only signal.</strong> Every state also changes weight, stroke, shape or text.</li>
    <li><strong>Accessibility is the floor.</strong> WCAG AA contrast, a visible 4px focus ring, 44px touch targets and real labels, on every component.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="built-h">
  <h2 id="built-h" class="type-h2">How it is built</h2>
  <p>The source of truth is a Figma file with three variable collections: Primitives (raw colors), Spacing, and Tokens (role names that point at a primitive, with five color modes, each in Light and Dark). A script turns an export of those variables into <code>tokens.css</code>. Components are plain HTML and CSS that read only from that file.</p>
  <p>Nothing here needs a framework to use. Copy the HTML from any component page, include the three stylesheets, and it works.</p>
</section>

